{"name":"Winding Path","hex":"#c6bba2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"winding","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["gerund of wind\n The act of winnowing (“subjecting food grain to a current of air to separate the grain from the chaff”).\n The act of blowing air through a wind instrument or  a horn to make a sound.","The act of winnowing (“subjecting food grain to a current of air to separate the grain from the chaff”)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Causing one to be breathless or out of breath.","Of a horn or wind instrument: blown to make a sound."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/winding"},{"term":"path","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trail for the use of, or worn by, pedestrians.","A course taken."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a path in, or on (something), or for (someone).","To navigate through a file system directory tree (to a desired file or folder)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/path"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electromagnetic coil","blocks":[{"text":"An electromagnetic coil is an electrical conductor such as a wire in the shape of a coil. Electromagnetic coils are used in electrical engineering, in applications where electric currents interact with magnetic fields, in devices such as electric motors, generators, inductors, electromagnets, transformers, sensor coils such as in medical MRI imaging machines. Either an electric current is passed through the wire of the coil to generate a magnetic field, or conversely, an external time-varying magnetic field through the interior of the coil generates an EMF (voltage) in the conductor."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Magnetic_field_of_loop_3.svg/330px-Magnetic_field_of_loop_3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_coil"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/